  "body": {
    "id": "thm:compatibility-frontier",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "The model class \\(MC(A,B)\\) is nonempty if and only if \\(-(1-p)r\\le a-b\\le p(1-r)\\), equivalently if and only if \\(cL\\le cU\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "MC",
      "A",
      "B",
      "p",
      "r",
      "a",
      "b",
      "cL",
      "cU"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:model-class",
      "def:mass-fiber",
      "lem:four-stratum-reduction",
      "thm:source-preserving-lift"
    ],
    "justification": "The theorem turns compatibility of two complete experiments with one SCM into two scalar inequalities.",
    "gap": "Gresele et al. formulate causal marginal compatibility and Duarte et al. test feasibility numerically, but no checked source gives this iff frontier for the confounded collider.",
    "consumer": "A data-fusion analyst can reject mutually inconsistent experimental tables before attempting causal optimization.",
    "proof_tex": "First suppose \\(MC(A,B)\\neq\\varnothing\\), and take \\(M\\in MC(A,B)\\).  By \\(\\text{lem:four-stratum-reduction}\\), its value \\(c=s(0,0)\\) satisfies \\(cL\\le c\\le cU\\); hence \\(cL\\le cU\\).\n\nConversely, if \\(cL\\le cU\\), choose \\(c=cL\\) and \\(d=0\\).  Since \\(w11>0\\), this pair belongs to \\(F(A,B)\\).  The source-preserving lift then supplies a model \\(M\\in MC(A,B)\\), so the class is nonempty.  Thus\n\\[\nMC(A,B)\\neq\\varnothing\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad cL\\le cU.\n\\]\n\nIt remains to simplify this condition.  Strict positivity and normalization imply\n\\[\n0<a<p=w00+w01,\\qquad 0<b<r=w00+w10.\n\\]\nFor \\(cL=\\max\\{0,a-w01,b-w10\\}\\) to be at most \\(cU=\\min\\{w00,a,b\\}\\), every lower candidate must not exceed every upper candidate.  All such comparisons are automatic from the preceding inequalities and nonnegativity, except\n\\[\na-w01\\le b\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad b-w10\\le a.\n\\]\nIndeed, for example, \\(a-w01\\le w00\\) follows from \\(a\\le w00+w01=p\\), while \\(b-w10\\le w00\\) follows from \\(b\\le w00+w10=r\\); comparisons with \\(a\\) or \\(b\\) themselves use \\(w01,w10\\ge0\\).  The two nonautomatic comparisons are equivalent to\n\\[\na-b\\le w01=p(1-r),\\qquad a-b\\ge-w10=-(1-p)r.\n\\]\nThis proves both stated equivalences, including equality cases.",
